  "worked_solution": "The instantaneous rate of change when $x=0$ is given by the value of the derivative ${f}^{\u2032}(x)$ at $x=0.$ \n\nFirst, we compute the derivative, using the fact that $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{tan}\u2061x)={sec}^{2}\u2061x$ and $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2})=2x.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{\u2032}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{tan}\u2061x-{x}^{2}) \\\\  & ={sec}^{2}\u2061x-2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating at $x=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{\u2032}(0) & ={sec}^{2}\u2061(0)-2\\cdot 0 \\\\  & ={(\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}\u2061(0)})}^{2}-0 \\\\  & ={(\\frac{1}{1})}^{2} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the instantaneous rate of change is $1.$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
